     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 4000 mg four times daily is 16,000 mg/day, about 230 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient. That is a toxic amount, and I know of no indication that supports it. Ibuprofen has a ceiling effect for analgesia and anti-inflammatory action, so a higher dose adds risk without adding benefit. The main risks are GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS effects. Entering it would put the patient at serious risk of harm.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter ibuprofen at the maximum labeled dose, 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day), or any lower dose you choose.\n- Enter a different or additional agent if the goal is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect. Examples are scheduled acetaminophen, or a different drug class that fits the indication.\n- Help you check the dose with a clinical pharmacist, if there is a specific reason you want a dose this high.\n\nTell me which of these you want and I'll enter the order."
      }
